  "summary": "The St. Cloud (MN) city council held a Sept. 21 work session on police use of Flock and Axon ALPRs, with the chief defending the technology while residents raised privacy and transparency concerns. The session followed Sherburne County's August decision to remove its Flock cameras and a June vandalism incident. Council members have not yet decided whether to hold a formal meeting on the issue.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "The St. Cloud Police Department owns five stationary Flock ALPRs, which were installed in summer 2024, and five stationary Axon ALPRs, which were installed this June",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    },
    {
      "quote": "On Aug. 13, Flock Safety lowered the limit for its own cameras from 30 days to seven.",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "But, in the steps they've taken related to mass surveillance cameras, they just keep going with it, and no one has checked in about the process. No one's talked about the legality of it.",
      "paragraph_idx": 22
    },
    {
      "quote": "he requested the biennial audit of the ALPR's use along with the contracts through a public information request. He said that was on Aug. 12, 41 days before the ALPR study session, and has not received anything from St. Cloud.",
      "paragraph_idx": 25
    }
  ],
